Parameter Default Meaning <code>table_name</code> <code>'claims'</code> <code>payments</code>, <code>claims</code>, <code>services</code>, <code>adjustments</code>, <code>provider_adjustments</code> or <code>diagnostics</code> <code>filename</code> <code>false</code> add a <code>filename</code> column with the path each row came from <code>version</code> the file's own force the built-in spec, <code>'5010'</code> or <code>'4010'</code>. By default each file uses the version it declares (5010 if none) <code>binary</code> <code>false</code> return text columns as <code>BLOB</code> (the raw bytes) instead of <code>VARCHAR</code> <code>ignore_errors</code> <code>false</code> report a file that is not an X12 interchange as a <code>diagnostics</code> row and go on <code>mask_key</code> none hide patient data: a key of at least 16 bytes. NULL is refused. See Sharing data without patient information <code>mask_dates</code> <code>'year'</code> with <code>mask_key</code>, how masked dates change: <code>'year'</code>, <code>'shift'</code> or <code>'keep'</code>. An error without <code>mask_key</code> <p>Forwarding a macro parameter</p> <p>Inside a macro with a literal path, forward the parameter with <code>=</code>, as in <code>table_name = tbl</code>. DuckDB does not substitute a macro parameter given with <code>:=</code> or <code>=&gt;</code>, so <code>read_835</code> refuses it when the macro is created.</p> <pre><code>CREATE MACRO remit_table(tbl) AS TABLE
    SELECT * FROM read_835('remits/a.835', table_name = tbl);
SELECT count(*) FROM remit_table('payments');
</code></pre> <p>To see the columns of a table, describe it on any file:</p> <pre><code>DESCRIBE SELECT * FROM read_835('remittance.835', table_name := 'claims');
</code></pre> <p>Tables and columns lists them all.</p>
